course: adult-life
lesson: 10
title: Moving house and renting a home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Families move house for many reasons: a new job, more room, a cheaper rent, or because a tenancy ends. Buying, selling or renting a home has many steps, and things can take months or fall through.
explain: An estate agent helps sell a house and shows people round; a survey checks it for problems; a mortgage from the bank pays for most of it. Renters sign a tenancy and pay a deposit that comes back if the home is left in good condition.
explain: Example: in a house in Brisbane, a "For Sale" board goes up out the front. Every Saturday the kids race to tidy their rooms before strangers come for a viewing, and weeks later Mum bursts in: "We've had an offer, but it's subject to survey!"
explain: Moving is one of the most stressful things grown-ups do, so they can be tired and snappy. Feeling sad about leaving a home is normal, even when the new place is nice.
fact: A "For Sale" board means the family has decided to sell the house, and selling usually takes months, not days.
fact: An estate agent helps sell houses, and viewings are when people who might buy come to look round.
fact: A tidy house helps buyers imagine living there and can sell faster or for more money.
fact: An offer "subject to survey" means the buyer wants an expert to check the house first, and may lower the offer or pull out if problems are found.
fact: A landlord is allowed to raise the rent, usually with warning, and a rise leaves less money for other things.
fact: A rental deposit is money held in case of damage, and it comes back if the home is left in good condition.
fact: A tenancy is the agreement to rent a home for a set time; at the end the landlord can offer a new one or ask the family to leave.
fact: "It's got potential" is a polite way of saying a house is not nice now but could be with work and money.
fact: A mortgage is a very big bank loan to buy a house, paid back monthly for 25 years or more with interest.
fact: Moving means packing everything, doing paperwork and saying goodbye all at once, so it is very stressful.
fact: A welcome card from new neighbours is a friendly hello, and getting on with neighbours makes life easier.
exercise: Dad says "The estate agent is bringing people round on Saturday." What is an estate agent? | A person whose job is to help sell houses | A builder who comes to repair the house before it is sold | A council worker who checks the bins | A bank worker who lends money
exercise: Why does the house need to be spotless for a viewing? | Buyers can imagine living there, so it may sell better | The estate agent gives marks for tidiness and writes them on the sign | The law says houses must be spotless | The buyers will clean it otherwise
exercise: A "For Sale" board goes up outside your house. What does it mean? | Your parents have decided to sell the house | The house next door is being knocked down next week | You are moving out tomorrow morning | The council has taken the house away
exercise: How long does selling a house usually take? | Months, not days | One afternoon | Exactly one week | Twenty years
exercise: Mum says "We've had an offer, but it's subject to survey." What happens next? | An expert checks the house for problems first | The buyer moves in the very next day with all their things | The family must leave the house tonight | The bank gives the family the money now
exercise: Why are grown-ups nervous until the survey is done? | Problems found may lower the offer or stop the sale | The surveyor might take some of the furniture away with him | The survey costs the family a million pounds | The survey decides which school you go to
exercise: The landlord's letter says "The rent is going up from next month." What does it mean for the family? | Less money for other things each month | The family now owns part of the house | The landlord will come and repaint the whole house | The family gets a refund next month
exercise: Mum says "We need to get the deposit back, so don't stick anything on the walls." Why? | Damage can be taken out of the deposit | Posters make the rent go up straight away | The landlord collects all the posters for himself | Sticky tape is against the law
exercise: Dad says "Our tenancy ends in June and we don't know if they'll renew it." What is a tenancy? | The agreement to rent a home for a set time | A loan from the bank to buy a home, paid back over years | A tax paid to the council each year | A party for new neighbours
exercise: Why do grown-ups start looking at other places before the tenancy ends? | They might be asked to leave, so they plan just in case | Renters must move every June by law, whatever happens | Looking at houses is a fun hobby | The landlord pays them to look
exercise: Looking round a house, Dad says "It's got potential." What does he mean? | It's not nice now, but could be with work and money | It is perfect and ready to move into with no work at all | It is too expensive to even think about | It is haunted and nobody wants it
exercise: In an advert, a "cosy" bedroom usually means what? | Small | Huge | Warm and new | Full of furniture
exercise: Mum and Dad say "We went to the bank about a mortgage." What is a mortgage? | A very big loan to buy a house, paid back over years | A gift from the bank for new families who need a home | The rent paid to a landlord each month | A fee for the estate agent's sign
exercise: Mum says "Moving house is one of the most stressful things you can do." Why? | Packing, paperwork and goodbyes all happen at once | Moving house is dangerous for children and their pets | New houses always have no heating | Moving costs nothing, which worries people
exercise: How can a child help during a move? | Pack and label their own box | Hide in the garden so they are not in the way | Unpack everything on the first night | Throw away things without asking
exercise: The new neighbours bring a card saying "Welcome to the street!" What is it? | A friendly hello from people nearby | A bill for living on the street | A warning to keep the noise down after dark | An invitation to sell the house
test: Your brother panics: "For Sale sign means we move tomorrow!!" Put him right. | Selling usually takes months, not days | He is right, you leave the very next morning | The sign means the house is falling down | The sign means someone else lives there now
test: Overheard on the phone: "Survey found damp and a dodgy roof, they've dropped the offer by ten grand." Why did the offer drop? | The survey found problems that cost money to fix | Damp makes houses worth more | The buyer just changed their mind about the colour of the walls | The estate agent wanted a bigger fee
test: Mum snaps "Not now, love, I've got forty boxes and the van's at eight!" Why is she snappy? | Moving is very stressful, and it calms down after | She is angry with you for something you did last week | She does not want to move at all | Snapping is required when moving house
test: Your sister stuck posters up with tape in the rented flat, and the paint came off. What might happen? | Money for repairs can be taken from the deposit | The landlord will pay the family a reward for the posters | The rent will go down next month | Nothing, because renters never pay for damage
test: Your mate says "Strangers looked in my cupboards at the viewing, so rude!" What's true? | Viewers look round to see the space, it's normal | Viewers are looking for things to steal from the house | Viewers are not allowed to open anything | The estate agent hides things in cupboards
test: Dad reads an advert aloud: "Charming, cosy, full of character, needs some updating." Mum laughs. Why? | Agent words often mean small, old and needing work | The house is clearly perfect and brand new inside | Charming means the house is enormous | Mum always laughs at houses
test: Mum on the phone: "Landlord says he's not renewing, we've got two months to find somewhere." What happens to the tenancy? | It ends, and the family must find another home | The family can stay forever anyway, whatever the landlord says | The landlord must give them the house | The rent goes down to keep them
test: Your cousin asks "Do people just pay for a house with cash from the shop?" What is usually true? | Most borrow with a mortgage and pay back over years | Everyone pays for a house all at once, in cash, on the day | The council gives houses to every family | Estate agents pay for the house themselves
test: The new neighbour says "Pop round any time, kids!" What should you do? | Not go into anyone's house without your parents knowing | Go in straight away on your own | Ignore the neighbour completely | Ask them for money
test: The sale falls through after three months. Dad sighs: "Back to square one." What is true? | Sales can fall through, and they just keep trying | The family has lost the house for good and must leave | They can never sell the house now | It means the house is worth nothing
test: You feel sad leaving the bedroom you grew up in, though the new house is bigger. Is that normal? | Yes, feeling sad about leaving a home is normal | No, you should only feel happy | No, only grown-ups feel sad when moving | Yes, but only if the new house is smaller
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises an offer subject to survey? | An offer conditional upon an expert inspection of the property | An offer which is binding irrespective of any defects that may be found | An offer made by the vendor to the purchaser | An offer which concludes the sale on the same day
exam: Identify the purpose of a deposit remitted at the commencement of a tenancy. | To secure the landlord against damage, being returnable if none is caused | To discharge the first year's rent in advance | To purchase a share in the ownership of the dwelling for the tenants | To remunerate the estate agent for viewings
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of a tenancy. | The landlord may offer a new tenancy or require the tenants to vacate | The tenants acquire ownership of the dwelling | The tenancy is automatically extended in perpetuity on identical terms for all parties | The rent is refunded in full to the tenants
exam: State the principal reason for which a dwelling is presented in a clean and orderly condition prior to a viewing. | Prospective purchasers may more readily envisage residing therein | The estate agent is legally obliged to award marks | Viewings are prohibited in untidy dwellings | Purchasers are required to clean the dwelling otherwise
